<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heian-kyō</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heian-kyō ('capital of peace and tranquillity') was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Emperor Kanmu</a> in 794, on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Laid out on a grid modelled on the Chinese capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, it remained the imperial seat for more than a thousand years and gave its name to the Heian period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heian-kyo.jpg" alt="Heian-kyō" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikiwikiyarou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daidairi_of_Heiankyo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
